WEBVTT
X-TIMESTAMP-MAP=LOCAL:00:00:00.000,MPEGTS:0

00:11.236 --> 00:14.036 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

00:18.916 --> 00:19.926 align:middle
Good afternoon.

00:20.926 --> 00:24.566 align:middle
As you know from our policy
statement released a short time

00:24.566 --> 00:28.786 align:middle
ago, the Federal Open Market
Committee reaffirmed the current

00:28.786 --> 00:33.156 align:middle
0 to 1/4 percent target range
for the federal funds rate.

00:33.706 --> 00:36.356 align:middle
Since the Committee met in July,

00:36.906 --> 00:39.576 align:middle
the pace of job gains
has been solid,

00:40.416 --> 00:42.686 align:middle
the unemployment
rate has declined,

00:43.576 --> 00:47.036 align:middle
and overall labor market
conditions have continued

00:47.036 --> 00:47.956 align:middle
to improve.

00:49.076 --> 00:51.216 align:middle
Inflation, however,
has continued

00:51.216 --> 00:53.616 align:middle
to run below our
longer-run objective,

00:54.426 --> 00:57.876 align:middle
partly reflecting declines
in energy and import prices.

00:59.006 --> 01:02.286 align:middle
While we still expect that the
downward pressure on inflation

01:02.336 --> 01:04.846 align:middle
from these factors
will fade over time,

01:05.516 --> 01:07.026 align:middle
recent global economic

01:07.026 --> 01:09.556 align:middle
and financial developments
are likely

01:09.596 --> 01:13.926 align:middle
to put further downward pressure
on inflation in the near term.

01:15.206 --> 01:19.286 align:middle
These developments may also
restrain U.S. activity somewhat

01:20.076 --> 01:23.796 align:middle
but have not led at this
point to a significant change

01:23.796 --> 01:26.796 align:middle
in the Committee's outlook
for the U.S. economy.

01:28.146 --> 01:30.316 align:middle
The Committee continues
to anticipate

01:30.316 --> 01:31.756 align:middle
that the first increase

01:31.756 --> 01:34.896 align:middle
in the federal funds
rate will be appropriate

01:35.206 --> 01:38.336 align:middle
when it has seen some further
improvement in the labor market

01:38.336 --> 01:42.746 align:middle
and is reasonably confident
that inflation will move back

01:42.746 --> 01:45.656 align:middle
to its 2 percent objective
over the medium term.

01:46.876 --> 01:49.716 align:middle
It remains the case that the
Committee will determine the

01:49.746 --> 01:53.646 align:middle
timing of the initial increase
based on its assessment

01:53.646 --> 01:56.836 align:middle
of the implications of
incoming information

01:56.836 --> 01:58.226 align:middle
for the economic outlook.

01:58.806 --> 02:01.026 align:middle
I will note that the importance

02:01.026 --> 02:03.886 align:middle
of the initial increase
should not be overstated:

02:04.726 --> 02:08.166 align:middle
The stance of monetary policy
will likely remain highly

02:08.316 --> 02:12.516 align:middle
accommodative for quite some
time after the initial increase

02:12.516 --> 02:14.816 align:middle
in the federal funds
rate in order

02:14.816 --> 02:18.246 align:middle
to support continued
progress toward our objectives

02:18.246 --> 02:21.556 align:middle
of maximum employment
and 2 percent inflation.

02:22.196 --> 02:25.316 align:middle
I will come back to
today's policy decision

02:25.316 --> 02:27.566 align:middle
in a few moments,
but first I would

02:27.566 --> 02:30.976 align:middle
like to review recent economic
developments and the outlook.

02:31.956 --> 02:34.846 align:middle
Smoothing through the
quarterly volatility,

02:35.476 --> 02:40.016 align:middle
U.S. real gross domestic product
is estimated to have expanded

02:40.306 --> 02:44.556 align:middle
at a 2-1/4 percent pace in
the first half of the year,

02:45.326 --> 02:49.026 align:middle
a notably stronger outcome
than expected in June,

02:49.306 --> 02:53.256 align:middle
when Committee participants had
submitted economic projections.

02:54.236 --> 02:57.066 align:middle
Continued job gains
and increases

02:57.066 --> 03:00.476 align:middle
in real disposable income have
supported household spending.

03:01.546 --> 03:04.586 align:middle
Growth in business fixed
investment was moderate,

03:05.176 --> 03:08.266 align:middle
held down in part by a
significant contraction

03:08.596 --> 03:12.516 align:middle
in oil drilling activity as
a result of the large drop

03:12.516 --> 03:14.676 align:middle
in oil prices over
the past year.

03:16.366 --> 03:19.286 align:middle
Moreover, net exports
were a substantial drag

03:19.286 --> 03:22.746 align:middle
on GDP growth during the
first half of the year,

03:23.376 --> 03:26.586 align:middle
reflecting the earlier
appreciation of the dollar

03:26.846 --> 03:28.436 align:middle
and weaker foreign demand.

03:29.456 --> 03:32.826 align:middle
The Committee continues
to expect a moderate pace

03:32.826 --> 03:36.566 align:middle
of overall GDP growth
even though the restraint

03:36.566 --> 03:39.576 align:middle
from net exports is likely
to persist for a time.

03:40.946 --> 03:43.136 align:middle
The labor market has
shown further progress

03:43.136 --> 03:46.846 align:middle
so far this year toward our
objective of maximum employment.

03:47.926 --> 03:49.416 align:middle
Over the past three months,

03:49.446 --> 03:52.666 align:middle
job gains averaged
220,000 per month.

03:53.466 --> 03:56.956 align:middle
The unemployment rate,
at 5.1 percent in August,

03:57.586 --> 04:01.296 align:middle
was down 0.4 percent from
the latest reading available

04:01.386 --> 04:03.306 align:middle
at the time of our June meeting,

04:04.256 --> 04:07.286 align:middle
although that decline was
accompanied by some reduction

04:07.286 --> 04:10.986 align:middle
in the labor force participation
rate over the same period.

04:12.186 --> 04:14.016 align:middle
A broader measure
of unemployment

04:14.016 --> 04:18.596 align:middle
that includes individuals who
want and are available to work

04:18.956 --> 04:22.746 align:middle
but have not actively
searched recently and people

04:22.746 --> 04:24.126 align:middle
who are working part time

04:24.126 --> 04:27.986 align:middle
but would rather work full
time has continued to improve.

04:28.936 --> 04:32.306 align:middle
That said, some cyclical
weakness likely remains:

04:33.016 --> 04:34.816 align:middle
While the unemployment
rate is close

04:34.816 --> 04:37.976 align:middle
to most FOMC participants'
estimates

04:38.036 --> 04:39.846 align:middle
of the longer-run normal level,

04:40.366 --> 04:43.276 align:middle
the participation rate
is still below estimates

04:43.276 --> 04:44.766 align:middle
of its underlying trend,

04:45.416 --> 04:48.816 align:middle
involuntary part-time
employment remains elevated,

04:49.446 --> 04:51.466 align:middle
and wage growth remains subdued.

04:52.806 --> 04:56.916 align:middle
Inflation has continued to run
below our 2 percent objective,

04:57.656 --> 05:01.216 align:middle
partly reflecting declines
in energy and import prices.

05:02.316 --> 05:05.946 align:middle
My colleagues and I continue
to expect that the effects

05:05.946 --> 05:09.196 align:middle
of these factors on
inflation will be transitory.

05:10.376 --> 05:14.126 align:middle
However, the recent
additional decline in oil prices

05:14.126 --> 05:17.646 align:middle
and further appreciation
of the dollar mean

05:17.646 --> 05:19.436 align:middle
that it will take
a bit more time

05:19.436 --> 05:21.606 align:middle
for these effects
to fully dissipate.

05:22.726 --> 05:24.966 align:middle
Accordingly, the
Committee anticipates

05:24.966 --> 05:28.396 align:middle
that inflation will remain
quite low in the coming months.

05:29.496 --> 05:33.256 align:middle
As these temporary effects
fade and, importantly,

05:33.256 --> 05:38.006 align:middle
as the labor market improves
further, we expect inflation

05:38.006 --> 05:41.076 align:middle
to move gradually back toward
our 2 percent objective.

05:42.176 --> 05:43.786 align:middle
Survey-based measures

05:43.786 --> 05:46.386 align:middle
of longer-term inflation
expectations have

05:46.386 --> 05:47.396 align:middle
remained stable.

05:48.326 --> 05:51.896 align:middle
However, the Committee has
taken note of recent declines

05:52.236 --> 05:55.416 align:middle
in market-based measures
of inflation compensation

05:55.416 --> 05:58.286 align:middle
and will continue
to monitor inflation

05:58.286 --> 05:59.576 align:middle
developments carefully.

06:01.366 --> 06:03.876 align:middle
This assessment of the
outlook is reflected

06:03.876 --> 06:07.066 align:middle
in the individual economic
projections submitted

06:07.066 --> 06:10.086 align:middle
for this meeting by
FOMC participants,

06:10.376 --> 06:12.546 align:middle
which now extend through 2018.

06:13.686 --> 06:16.306 align:middle
As announced in the minutes
from our July meeting,

06:16.636 --> 06:20.286 align:middle
we are also introducing a modest
enhancement to the Summary

06:20.286 --> 06:24.836 align:middle
of Economic Projections by
publishing the median projection

06:25.036 --> 06:27.616 align:middle
across FOMC participants.

06:28.446 --> 06:32.066 align:middle
These medians provide a
concise summary statistic

06:32.336 --> 06:34.216 align:middle
of participants' perspectives.

06:35.076 --> 06:38.736 align:middle
They should not, however, be
interpreted as a collective view

06:38.736 --> 06:40.466 align:middle
or a Committee forecast.

06:41.226 --> 06:45.276 align:middle
As always, each participant's
projections are conditioned

06:45.276 --> 06:49.606 align:middle
on his or her own view of
appropriate monetary policy.

06:50.986 --> 06:54.186 align:middle
Reflecting upward revisions
for the first half of the year,

06:54.766 --> 06:57.126 align:middle
participants increased
their projections

06:57.126 --> 06:58.986 align:middle
for economic growth this year,

06:59.426 --> 07:02.576 align:middle
compared with the
projections made in conjunction

07:02.576 --> 07:04.376 align:middle
with the June FOMC meeting.

07:05.486 --> 07:09.676 align:middle
The median growth projection
is 2.1 percent for this year

07:10.166 --> 07:13.576 align:middle
and rises to 2.3
percent in 2016,

07:14.376 --> 07:16.406 align:middle
somewhat above the
median estimate

07:16.406 --> 07:18.536 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal growth rate.

07:19.686 --> 07:23.346 align:middle
Thereafter, the median growth
projection declines toward its

07:23.346 --> 07:24.286 align:middle
longer-run rate.

07:25.716 --> 07:28.506 align:middle
The unemployment rate
projections are a bit lower

07:28.506 --> 07:29.396 align:middle
than in June.

07:30.316 --> 07:31.656 align:middle
At the end of this year,

07:31.656 --> 07:35.596 align:middle
the median unemployment rate
projection stands at 5 percent,

07:36.716 --> 07:41.536 align:middle
down 0.3 percent from June and
close to the median estimate

07:41.536 --> 07:43.816 align:middle
of the longer-run
normal unemployment rate.

07:45.056 --> 07:49.326 align:middle
Committee participants generally
see the unemployment rate

07:49.686 --> 07:53.336 align:middle
declining a little further next
year and then leveling out.

07:54.156 --> 07:57.956 align:middle
Finally, FOMC participants
project inflation

07:57.956 --> 08:02.286 align:middle
to be very low this year,
largely reflecting lower energy

08:02.286 --> 08:04.216 align:middle
and non-energy import prices.

08:05.376 --> 08:08.946 align:middle
As the transitory factors
holding down inflation abate

08:09.296 --> 08:12.456 align:middle
and labor market
conditions continue to firm,

08:13.126 --> 08:15.846 align:middle
the median inflation
projection rises

08:16.226 --> 08:21.596 align:middle
from just 0.4 percent this
year to 1.7 percent next year

08:21.806 --> 08:23.926 align:middle
and reaches 2 percent in 2018.

08:24.816 --> 08:28.896 align:middle
The path of the median inflation
projections is a bit lower

08:28.896 --> 08:29.706 align:middle
than in June.

08:30.256 --> 08:34.906 align:middle
The outlook abroad appears
to have become more uncertain

08:34.906 --> 08:38.746 align:middle
of late, and heightened
concerns about growth in China

08:38.746 --> 08:42.796 align:middle
and other emerging
market economies have led

08:42.796 --> 08:45.646 align:middle
to notable volatility
in financial markets.

08:47.796 --> 08:49.816 align:middle
Developments since
our July meeting--

08:50.866 --> 08:55.226 align:middle
including the drop in equity
prices, the further appreciation

08:55.226 --> 08:58.726 align:middle
of the dollar, and a
widening in risk spreads--

08:59.256 --> 09:03.006 align:middle
have tightened overall financial
conditions to some extent.

09:04.256 --> 09:08.666 align:middle
These developments may restrain
U.S. economic activity somewhat

09:09.446 --> 09:12.156 align:middle
and are likely to put
further downward pressure

09:12.156 --> 09:14.066 align:middle
on inflation in the near term.

09:15.326 --> 09:19.256 align:middle
Given the significant economic
and financial interconnections

09:19.286 --> 09:22.556 align:middle
between the United States
and the rest of the world,

09:23.186 --> 09:26.066 align:middle
the situation abroad
bears close watching.

09:27.646 --> 09:31.366 align:middle
Returning to monetary
policy, we recognize

09:31.366 --> 09:33.936 align:middle
that there has been
a great deal of focus

09:33.936 --> 09:35.786 align:middle
on today's policy decision.

09:37.076 --> 09:41.276 align:middle
The recovery from the Great
Recession has advanced

09:41.276 --> 09:46.106 align:middle
sufficiently far and domestic
spending appears sufficiently

09:46.106 --> 09:50.196 align:middle
robust that an argument
can be made for a rise

09:50.196 --> 09:52.046 align:middle
in interest rates at this time.

09:53.436 --> 09:55.976 align:middle
We discussed this
possibility at our meeting.

09:57.116 --> 10:00.886 align:middle
However, in light of the
heightened uncertainties abroad

10:01.536 --> 10:05.256 align:middle
and a slightly softer
expected path for inflation,

10:06.116 --> 10:08.596 align:middle
the Committee judged
it appropriate to wait

10:08.596 --> 10:12.726 align:middle
for more evidence, including
some further improvement

10:12.726 --> 10:16.456 align:middle
in the labor market, to
bolster its confidence

10:16.456 --> 10:19.646 align:middle
that inflation will rise to
2 percent in the medium term.

10:20.986 --> 10:24.426 align:middle
Now, I do not want to
overplay the implications

10:24.426 --> 10:26.236 align:middle
of these recent developments,

10:27.056 --> 10:30.316 align:middle
which have not fundamentally
altered our outlook.

10:31.536 --> 10:34.016 align:middle
The economy has been
performing well,

10:34.386 --> 10:37.046 align:middle
and we expect it to
continue to do so.

10:38.546 --> 10:42.066 align:middle
As I noted earlier, it remains
the case that the timing

10:42.066 --> 10:45.746 align:middle
of the initial increase in the
federal funds rate will depend

10:45.746 --> 10:48.336 align:middle
on the Committee's
assessment of the implications

10:48.796 --> 10:51.576 align:middle
of incoming information
for the economic outlook.

10:52.206 --> 10:55.966 align:middle
To be clear, our
decision will not hinge

10:55.966 --> 10:58.346 align:middle
on any particular data release

10:58.896 --> 11:02.076 align:middle
or on day-to-day movements
in financial markets.

11:02.976 --> 11:06.866 align:middle
Instead, the decision will
depend on a wide range

11:06.866 --> 11:11.186 align:middle
of economic and financial
indicators and our assessment

11:11.186 --> 11:14.156 align:middle
of their cumulative
implications for actual

11:14.406 --> 11:16.936 align:middle
and expected progress
toward our objectives.

11:17.996 --> 11:21.176 align:middle
Let me again emphasize
that the specific timing

11:21.176 --> 11:23.546 align:middle
of the initial increase
in the target range

11:23.686 --> 11:27.316 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate is far less important

11:27.316 --> 11:31.666 align:middle
for the economy than the entire
expected path of interest rates.

11:32.136 --> 11:35.156 align:middle
And once we begin to
remove policy accommodation,

11:35.616 --> 11:39.776 align:middle
we continue to expect that
economic conditions will evolve

11:39.816 --> 11:43.166 align:middle
in a manner that will
warrant only gradual increases

11:43.426 --> 11:45.146 align:middle
in the target federal
funds rate.

11:45.916 --> 11:48.806 align:middle
Compared with the
projections made in June,

11:49.366 --> 11:53.216 align:middle
many FOMC participants
lowered somewhat their paths

11:53.516 --> 11:57.386 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate, including estimates

11:57.386 --> 11:59.146 align:middle
of the longer-run normal level.

12:00.526 --> 12:02.966 align:middle
Most participants
continue to expect

12:02.966 --> 12:06.396 align:middle
that economic conditions
will make it appropriate

12:06.396 --> 12:08.036 align:middle
to raise the target range

12:08.036 --> 12:11.066 align:middle
for the federal funds
rate later this year,

12:12.106 --> 12:14.446 align:middle
although four participants
now expect

12:14.446 --> 12:18.826 align:middle
that such conditions will not be
seen until next year or later.

12:20.146 --> 12:23.766 align:middle
The median projection for
the federal funds rate rises

12:23.766 --> 12:29.676 align:middle
to about 1-1/2 percent in
late 2016, 2-1/2 percent

12:29.676 --> 12:34.196 align:middle
in late 2017, and
3-1/2 percent in 2018.

12:34.196 --> 12:38.996 align:middle
In 2016 and 2017,
the medians are

12:38.996 --> 12:43.456 align:middle
about 1/4 percentage point
below those projected in June.

12:44.486 --> 12:49.146 align:middle
The median projected rate in
2017 remains below the rate

12:49.146 --> 12:51.986 align:middle
that most participants
expect to prevail

12:51.986 --> 12:54.916 align:middle
in the longer run
despite the fact

12:54.916 --> 12:58.856 align:middle
that the median projection has
the unemployment rate slightly

12:58.856 --> 13:03.236 align:middle
below its longer-run normal
level and inflation close

13:03.236 --> 13:04.896 align:middle
to our 2 percent objective.

13:06.026 --> 13:08.966 align:middle
Participants provided a
number of explanations

13:09.166 --> 13:11.876 align:middle
for their low federal
funds rate projections.

13:12.656 --> 13:15.776 align:middle
These included, in
particular, the residual effects

13:15.776 --> 13:19.686 align:middle
of the financial crisis,
which are likely to continue

13:19.686 --> 13:21.886 align:middle
to constrain spending
for some time,

13:22.726 --> 13:24.806 align:middle
as well as headwinds
from abroad.

13:25.746 --> 13:30.256 align:middle
But the restraining influence of
these factors on real activity--

13:30.876 --> 13:34.166 align:middle
as the restraining
influence of these factors

13:34.596 --> 13:37.056 align:middle
on real activity
dissipates further,

13:37.436 --> 13:41.036 align:middle
most participants expect the
federal funds rate to move

13:41.036 --> 13:44.986 align:middle
to its longer-run normal
level by the end of 2018.

13:46.256 --> 13:48.806 align:middle
I'd like to underscore
that the forecasts

13:48.806 --> 13:53.156 align:middle
of the appropriate path of the
federal funds rate, as usual,

13:53.536 --> 13:57.366 align:middle
are conditional on participants'
individual projections

13:57.826 --> 14:01.456 align:middle
of the most likely outcomes for
economic growth, employment,

14:01.456 --> 14:03.086 align:middle
inflation, and other factors.

14:04.046 --> 14:08.266 align:middle
But our actual policy
actions over time will depend

14:08.566 --> 14:10.996 align:middle
on how economic conditions
evolve,

14:11.486 --> 14:13.106 align:middle
which is quite uncertain.

14:13.876 --> 14:16.476 align:middle
If the expansion proves
to be more vigorous

14:16.476 --> 14:20.016 align:middle
than currently anticipated
and inflation moves higher

14:20.016 --> 14:23.676 align:middle
than expected, then the
appropriate path would likely

14:23.676 --> 14:26.576 align:middle
follow a steeper and
higher trajectory.

14:27.726 --> 14:30.316 align:middle
Conversely, if conditions
were to prove weaker,

14:30.906 --> 14:34.486 align:middle
then the appropriate trajectory
would be lower and less steep.

14:35.386 --> 14:38.206 align:middle
Finally, the Committee
will continue its policy

14:38.206 --> 14:42.436 align:middle
of reinvesting proceeds from
maturing Treasury securities

14:42.436 --> 14:45.066 align:middle
and principal payments
from agency debt

14:45.066 --> 14:46.916 align:middle
and mortgage-backed securities.

14:47.606 --> 14:49.326 align:middle
The Committee's sizable holdings

14:49.326 --> 14:53.346 align:middle
of longer-term securities should
help maintain accommodative

14:53.346 --> 14:57.186 align:middle
financial conditions and
promote further progress toward

14:57.186 --> 14:58.136 align:middle
our objectives.

14:59.086 --> 14:59.666 align:middle
Thank you.

14:59.666 --> 15:00.576 align:middle
Let me stop there.

15:00.576 --> 15:02.466 align:middle
I'll be happy to
take your questions.

15:07.086 --> 15:07.816 align:middle
STEVE LIESMAN.

15:07.936 --> 15:10.136 align:middle
Steve Liesman, CNBC.

15:10.136 --> 15:15.046 align:middle
Madam Chair, this notion
of uncertainty in economic

15:15.046 --> 15:17.236 align:middle
and global developments--

15:18.156 --> 15:22.176 align:middle
is it fair to say that it could
be many months before those

15:22.176 --> 15:23.446 align:middle
global developments
work their way

15:23.446 --> 15:25.046 align:middle
through the U.S. economic data,

15:25.576 --> 15:28.236 align:middle
and that you would not have the
certainty that you're looking

15:28.236 --> 15:30.726 align:middle
for to raise interest
rates for many months

15:30.726 --> 15:31.896 align:middle
and perhaps well into next year?

15:32.146 --> 15:32.896 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

15:33.096 --> 15:37.396 align:middle
Well, Steve, I think you can
see from the SEP projections

15:37.826 --> 15:41.836 align:middle
that most participants
continued to think

15:42.346 --> 15:45.586 align:middle
that economic conditions
will call for

15:45.586 --> 15:49.246 align:middle
or make appropriate an increase
in the federal funds rate

15:49.486 --> 15:50.866 align:middle
by the end of this year.

15:51.656 --> 15:56.666 align:middle
Four participants moved their
projections into 2016 or later,

15:57.126 --> 15:58.876 align:middle
but the great majority

15:58.876 --> 16:01.976 align:middle
of participants continued
to hold that view.

16:02.446 --> 16:05.276 align:middle
And, of course, there will
always be uncertainty.

16:05.276 --> 16:09.646 align:middle
We can't expect that uncertainty
to be fully resolved.

16:10.206 --> 16:13.676 align:middle
But, in light of the
developments that we have seen

16:13.676 --> 16:16.716 align:middle
and the impacts on
financial markets,

16:16.856 --> 16:20.166 align:middle
we want to take a
little bit more time

16:20.356 --> 16:24.296 align:middle
to evaluate the likely
impacts on the United States.

16:24.296 --> 16:25.946 align:middle
And, as I mentioned,

16:26.156 --> 16:30.466 align:middle
the inflation outlook
has softened slightly.

16:30.466 --> 16:33.556 align:middle
We've had some further
developments--

16:33.556 --> 16:37.386 align:middle
namely, lower oil prices
and a further appreciation

16:37.386 --> 16:41.056 align:middle
of the dollar-- that have
put some downward pressure

16:41.056 --> 16:43.116 align:middle
in the near term on inflation.

16:43.576 --> 16:46.606 align:middle
Now, we fully expect
those further effects,

16:46.606 --> 16:49.946 align:middle
like the earlier
moves in the dollar

16:50.206 --> 16:53.476 align:middle
and in oil prices,
to be transitory.

16:53.866 --> 16:57.876 align:middle
But there is a little bit of
downward pressure on inflation,

16:58.246 --> 17:02.936 align:middle
and we would like to see
some further developments.

17:02.936 --> 17:06.756 align:middle
And this, importantly, could
include-- is likely to include--

17:07.006 --> 17:09.136 align:middle
further improvements
in the labor market

17:09.456 --> 17:11.766 align:middle
that would bolster
our confidence

17:11.766 --> 17:15.226 align:middle
that inflation will move back to
2 percent over the medium term.

17:15.736 --> 17:18.936 align:middle
SAM FLEMING.

17:18.936 --> 17:21.416 align:middle
Sam Fleming from
the Financial Times.

17:21.876 --> 17:25.106 align:middle
Could I ask about the next
meeting, which is in October?

17:25.106 --> 17:28.846 align:middle
Do you view that as a live
meeting even though there isn't

17:28.846 --> 17:31.316 align:middle
going to be a scheduled press
conference at that time?

17:31.316 --> 17:33.916 align:middle
And what kind of developments
would you need to see

17:33.916 --> 17:36.506 align:middle
to be confident in
moving in the near term?

17:36.506 --> 17:38.546 align:middle
Is it more important,
the developments

17:38.546 --> 17:40.366 align:middle
in the financial markets,
or is it more to do

17:40.366 --> 17:41.766 align:middle
with the upcoming data?

17:41.966 --> 17:42.496 align:middle
Thanks very much.

17:43.586 --> 17:44.156 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

17:44.406 --> 17:48.516 align:middle
So, as I've said before, every
meeting is a live meeting

17:48.516 --> 17:54.486 align:middle
where the Committee can
make a decision to move

17:54.556 --> 17:57.216 align:middle
to change our target for
the federal funds rate.

17:57.546 --> 17:59.736 align:middle
That certainly includes October.

18:00.666 --> 18:05.386 align:middle
As you know, and I've stressed
previously, were we to decide

18:05.436 --> 18:09.796 align:middle
to do that, we would
call a press briefing,

18:10.076 --> 18:15.686 align:middle
and you've participated in
an exercise to make sure

18:15.686 --> 18:17.746 align:middle
that you would know
how to participate

18:17.746 --> 18:20.586 align:middle
in that press briefing
should it happen.

18:20.886 --> 18:25.066 align:middle
So, yes, October
remains a possibility.

18:25.646 --> 18:29.506 align:middle
And we will be looking
at incoming developments,

18:29.506 --> 18:35.686 align:middle
both financial and economic,
to try to make sure we feel,

18:36.106 --> 18:39.536 align:middle
really, that the U.S.
economy is doing well.

18:39.966 --> 18:41.416 align:middle
You know, I want to emphasize,

18:41.466 --> 18:45.216 align:middle
domestic developments
have been strong.

18:45.476 --> 18:50.146 align:middle
We see domestic demand
growing at a solid pace,

18:50.146 --> 18:53.206 align:middle
the labor market
continuing to improve.

18:53.676 --> 18:56.296 align:middle
Of course, we will
watch incoming data

18:56.296 --> 19:01.606 align:middle
to confirm our expectation
that that will continue.

19:01.956 --> 19:05.916 align:middle
And we, of course, will
watch global financial

19:05.916 --> 19:07.496 align:middle
and economic developments.

19:07.766 --> 19:11.566 align:middle
I can't give you a recipe for
exactly what we're looking

19:11.566 --> 19:16.196 align:middle
to see, but, as we say, we want
to see continued improvement

19:16.196 --> 19:18.176 align:middle
in the labor market,
and we would

19:18.176 --> 19:20.376 align:middle
like to bolster our confidence

19:20.466 --> 19:22.826 align:middle
that inflation will
move back to 2 percent.

19:23.046 --> 19:24.826 align:middle
And, of course, a
further improvement

19:24.826 --> 19:27.266 align:middle
in the labor market
does serve that purpose.

19:27.506 --> 19:31.126 align:middle
There could be other things we
would see that could bolster

19:31.126 --> 19:33.286 align:middle
that confidence, but
further improvement

19:33.286 --> 19:36.146 align:middle
in the labor market
will serve to do that.

19:39.746 --> 19:40.936 align:middle
JIM PUZZANGHERA.

19:41.536 --> 19:43.256 align:middle
Hi. Jim Puzzanghera
from the L.A. Times.

19:43.626 --> 19:46.696 align:middle
There were a group of protesters
out here before your meeting.

19:46.696 --> 19:49.216 align:middle
There was a similar
group at Jackson Hole.

19:49.556 --> 19:53.646 align:middle
They and others have
warned the Fed not

19:53.646 --> 19:54.906 align:middle
to raise the rate because--

19:55.046 --> 19:58.696 align:middle
out of concern that the labor
market is not fully healed,

19:58.816 --> 20:00.556 align:middle
wages haven't risen fast enough.

20:00.896 --> 20:04.126 align:middle
What impact, if any, has that
had on you and your colleagues

20:04.126 --> 20:05.046 align:middle
in your decision today?

20:06.046 --> 20:06.426 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

20:07.206 --> 20:11.556 align:middle
So, we have been receiving
advice from a large number

20:11.556 --> 20:14.076 align:middle
of economists and
interested groups,

20:14.466 --> 20:17.006 align:middle
and that's of course
appropriate,

20:17.006 --> 20:21.966 align:middle
and we value hearing
the opinions of many--

20:22.166 --> 20:24.536 align:middle
you know, many different
groups and individuals

20:24.576 --> 20:26.346 align:middle
with different perspectives.

20:26.406 --> 20:31.026 align:middle
But, look, at the end of the
day, it's the Committee's job

20:31.116 --> 20:35.946 align:middle
to come together to analyze
the data that we have

20:35.996 --> 20:41.046 align:middle
on the economy, to decide
how it affects the outlook,

20:41.376 --> 20:44.946 align:middle
and to try to deliberate and
arrive at a Committee judgment

20:45.206 --> 20:47.756 align:middle
about the appropriate
path of policy,

20:48.146 --> 20:50.676 align:middle
and that's what we did today.

20:50.886 --> 20:54.086 align:middle
As I said, although we are close

20:54.126 --> 20:56.556 align:middle
to many participant's
[estimates]

20:56.786 --> 21:01.016 align:middle
and the median estimate of
the longer-run normal rate

21:01.016 --> 21:04.666 align:middle
of unemployment, at
least my own judgment--

21:04.666 --> 21:06.736 align:middle
and this has been
true for a long time--

21:07.036 --> 21:09.846 align:middle
is that there are
additional margins of slack,

21:10.356 --> 21:14.376 align:middle
particularly relating
to very high levels

21:14.896 --> 21:19.146 align:middle
of part-time involuntary
employment

21:19.436 --> 21:23.446 align:middle
and labor force participation,
that suggest that,

21:23.446 --> 21:25.056 align:middle
at least to some extent,

21:25.286 --> 21:29.826 align:middle
the standard unemployment
rate understates the degree

21:29.826 --> 21:31.656 align:middle
of slack in the labor market.

21:31.866 --> 21:33.486 align:middle
But we are getting closer.

21:33.716 --> 21:35.846 align:middle
The labor market has improved.

21:36.126 --> 21:40.776 align:middle
And, as I've said in the
past, we don't want to wait

21:40.856 --> 21:43.786 align:middle
until we've fully met
both of our objectives

21:44.096 --> 21:48.066 align:middle
to begin the process of
tightening policy given the lags

21:48.326 --> 21:50.366 align:middle
in the operation
of monetary policy.

21:51.356 --> 21:52.146 align:middle
KATE DAVIDSON.

21:55.476 --> 21:56.136 align:middle
Thank you.

21:56.136 --> 21:57.676 align:middle
Kate Davidson from the
Wall Street Journal.

21:58.096 --> 22:00.246 align:middle
Madam Chair, do you think,
over the past two months

22:00.246 --> 22:02.506 align:middle
since your last meeting,
that you've gotten closer to

22:02.506 --> 22:05.356 align:middle
or further away from the
Fed's inflation goals?

22:05.636 --> 22:06.876 align:middle
And then, separately,

22:07.166 --> 22:10.296 align:middle
you've received a
congressional subpoena relating

22:10.296 --> 22:11.886 align:middle
to the disclosure of information

22:11.886 --> 22:14.096 align:middle
from the Fed's September
2012 policy meeting.

22:14.096 --> 22:16.076 align:middle
Are you any closer to
complying with that?

22:16.076 --> 22:18.126 align:middle
Have you turned over any
new information to Congress?

22:18.406 --> 22:19.096 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

22:20.976 --> 22:24.736 align:middle
So, your first question
was, have we come closer

22:24.736 --> 22:27.416 align:middle
or moved further away
from our inflation goal.

22:27.546 --> 22:31.876 align:middle
So we've used the same language,
which is that we expect

22:31.876 --> 22:35.226 align:middle
to achieve our 2 present
goal over the medium term,

22:35.876 --> 22:41.306 align:middle
and I would say, although we--
as we say in our statement,

22:41.556 --> 22:47.286 align:middle
recent developments seem likely
to put some downward pressure.

22:47.286 --> 22:50.286 align:middle
We're, after all, way
below our inflation target.

22:51.136 --> 22:56.146 align:middle
But an important reason
for that is that declines

22:56.146 --> 23:00.106 align:middle
in import prices reflecting
the appreciation of the dollar

23:00.446 --> 23:03.846 align:middle
and declines in energy
prices are holding

23:03.846 --> 23:06.836 align:middle
down inflation well
below our target

23:06.836 --> 23:09.926 align:middle
and well below core inflation.

23:11.276 --> 23:14.156 align:middle
We expect those effects
to be transitory,

23:14.156 --> 23:16.926 align:middle
and with well-anchored
inflation expectations,

23:17.176 --> 23:21.106 align:middle
we expect inflation to
move back to 2 percent.

23:21.586 --> 23:25.116 align:middle
Now, in the intermeeting period,

23:25.116 --> 23:29.486 align:middle
we have seen some further
appreciation of the dollar

23:29.486 --> 23:32.396 align:middle
and some further downward
pressure on energy prices,

23:32.866 --> 23:37.856 align:middle
and that creates a bit of
further drag on inflation

23:38.226 --> 23:41.326 align:middle
that I would view
as transitory--

23:41.376 --> 23:43.916 align:middle
is very likely to be transitory.

23:44.286 --> 23:47.826 align:middle
So I continue and the
Committee continues to expect

23:48.066 --> 23:50.606 align:middle
that inflation will
move back to 2 percent,

23:50.726 --> 23:53.506 align:middle
so this should be a small thing.

23:55.196 --> 23:59.546 align:middle
And, in the meantime, the labor
market has continued to improve,

24:00.246 --> 24:02.676 align:middle
so a tighter labor market,

24:02.676 --> 24:07.086 align:middle
a labor market moving
toward full employment,

24:07.496 --> 24:11.386 align:middle
is one that historically has
generated upward pressure

24:11.386 --> 24:12.206 align:middle
on inflation.

24:12.706 --> 24:16.066 align:middle
So that bolsters my
confidence in inflation.

24:16.416 --> 24:18.786 align:middle
On the other hand,
we've had a long period

24:19.156 --> 24:22.376 align:middle
in which inflation has been
running below our objective.

24:22.766 --> 24:26.876 align:middle
I consider it, and the Committee
does, very important that we--

24:28.866 --> 24:32.626 align:middle
we achieve our inflation
objective and defend

24:32.626 --> 24:35.856 align:middle
against inflation
that is persistently

24:35.856 --> 24:37.566 align:middle
above our inflation objective

24:37.566 --> 24:41.336 align:middle
and also persistently below
our inflation objective.

24:41.826 --> 24:46.186 align:middle
And we want to have not only an
expectation, but a good degree

24:46.186 --> 24:48.176 align:middle
of confidence that
that will occur.

24:48.706 --> 24:51.476 align:middle
We did take note in the
statement of a decline

24:51.476 --> 24:57.086 align:middle
in inflation compensation, and
it's hard to get a direct read

24:57.146 --> 25:00.856 align:middle
on inflation expectations
out of these measures.

25:01.336 --> 25:06.276 align:middle
They can be pushed down by
factors pertaining to liquidity

25:06.276 --> 25:09.756 align:middle
in the Treasury--
in the TIPs market

25:09.756 --> 25:13.186 align:middle
and other issues
pertaining to risk premia,

25:13.816 --> 25:16.556 align:middle
but we have taken note
of that, and I would say

25:16.556 --> 25:20.006 align:middle
that that's something that
has caught our attention

25:20.006 --> 25:23.886 align:middle
and is a factor that
we're watching.

25:23.886 --> 25:27.446 align:middle
So we'd like to have a
little bit more confidence,

25:27.476 --> 25:31.516 align:middle
but I would not interpret
developments during the

25:31.516 --> 25:32.896 align:middle
intermeeting period

25:32.896 --> 25:35.916 align:middle
as significantly
undermining confidence.

25:35.916 --> 25:41.156 align:middle
And the labor market is really
important that as it continues

25:41.156 --> 25:44.666 align:middle
to improve-- it has, and we
want to see it improve further--

25:44.996 --> 25:46.716 align:middle
that serves to bolster
confidence.

25:48.796 --> 25:53.216 align:middle
Oh, I'm sorry, you asked
about the September 2012 leak.

25:53.516 --> 25:55.286 align:middle
We are working very closely

25:55.286 --> 25:59.716 align:middle
with House Financial Services
Committee that's requested

25:59.716 --> 26:03.216 align:middle
information to satisfy
their request.

26:03.216 --> 26:05.856 align:middle
We're working very
closely with them.

26:06.876 --> 26:08.186 align:middle
BINYAMIN APPELBAUM.

26:09.796 --> 26:11.896 align:middle
Binya Appelbaum,
the New York Times.

26:12.306 --> 26:14.816 align:middle
The economic projections that
you all released today show

26:15.176 --> 26:17.846 align:middle
that Committee members expect
roughly a three-year period

26:17.956 --> 26:19.546 align:middle
in which the unemployment
rate will be

26:19.546 --> 26:21.736 align:middle
at its lowest sustainable
level and

26:21.736 --> 26:24.316 align:middle
yet inflation will not
rise above 2 percent.

26:24.696 --> 26:25.716 align:middle
That seems extraordinary.

26:25.716 --> 26:26.886 align:middle
Could you talk about why,

26:27.116 --> 26:29.526 align:middle
as a moment ago you said we
would expect inflationary

26:29.526 --> 26:31.386 align:middle
pressures when unemployment
is at a low level?

26:31.736 --> 26:35.066 align:middle
Why is inflation going
to be so weak for so long

26:35.066 --> 26:38.166 align:middle
under those circumstances,
and does it indicate--

26:38.166 --> 26:40.006 align:middle
when you are projecting
that basically much

26:40.006 --> 26:41.206 align:middle
of a decade will have passed

26:41.206 --> 26:43.446 align:middle
without the Fed reaching
its inflation target--

26:43.766 --> 26:45.796 align:middle
does it indicate that you
have failed to do enough

26:46.096 --> 26:48.276 align:middle
to revive this economy
in recent years?

26:48.276 --> 26:48.836 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

26:50.346 --> 26:53.186 align:middle
Well, we have been
very focused, Binya,

26:53.186 --> 26:57.446 align:middle
on doing everything we
can to revive this economy

26:57.446 --> 27:01.726 align:middle
and to achieve our maximum
employment objective.

27:02.076 --> 27:08.766 align:middle
And after we took the funds
rate down to zero, as you know,

27:09.086 --> 27:13.086 align:middle
we put in place a number of
other extraordinary measures--

27:13.406 --> 27:18.096 align:middle
including forward guidance and
large-scale asset purchases--

27:18.386 --> 27:21.396 align:middle
in order to speed the recovery

27:21.396 --> 27:26.096 align:middle
and attain both our
inflation objective

27:26.096 --> 27:28.636 align:middle
and our maximum employment
objective.

27:29.196 --> 27:33.326 align:middle
And, I mean, when you look
at the projection, you see,

27:33.766 --> 27:37.516 align:middle
as you mentioned, that
we see sufficient growth

27:37.596 --> 27:39.586 align:middle
to push the unemployment rate.

27:39.806 --> 27:44.966 align:middle
It's already very close
to participants' estimates

27:44.966 --> 27:47.416 align:middle
of its longer-run normal level.

27:48.516 --> 27:51.876 align:middle
We expect the unemployment
rate to fall slightly--

27:52.126 --> 27:54.186 align:middle
or, at least, participants
project

27:54.186 --> 27:57.306 align:middle
that it will fall
slightly below that level.

27:57.566 --> 28:02.276 align:middle
As that occurs, we would expect
labor force participation,

28:02.576 --> 28:08.576 align:middle
the cyclical component of
that, to diminish over time,

28:08.996 --> 28:13.696 align:middle
and we would hope to see
some decline in the portion

28:13.696 --> 28:16.946 align:middle
of slack that's reflected
in high levels

28:17.566 --> 28:20.996 align:middle
of part-time involuntary
employment.

28:21.396 --> 28:26.836 align:middle
Now, inflation is going back
in our projection to 2 percent.

28:27.156 --> 28:29.526 align:middle
It takes to 2018 to get there.

28:29.526 --> 28:33.056 align:middle
It's awfully close in 2017,

28:33.056 --> 28:37.206 align:middle
and it's not terribly
far away even next year.

28:37.616 --> 28:44.396 align:middle
We have very large drags from
import prices and energy prices,

28:44.756 --> 28:49.856 align:middle
and over the next year or so,
those things should dissipate.

28:50.286 --> 28:56.446 align:middle
And the behavior of inflation
should mainly-- if we--

28:56.446 --> 28:59.906 align:middle
if our understanding of the
inflationary process is correct

29:00.316 --> 29:04.136 align:middle
and if inflation expectations
are well anchored at 2,

29:04.136 --> 29:10.276 align:middle
which I believe they are, as
the labor market heals and as

29:10.276 --> 29:13.916 align:middle
that healing progresses, we
will see further upward pressure

29:13.916 --> 29:14.726 align:middle
on inflation.

29:15.136 --> 29:16.316 align:middle
That's what we expect.

29:16.316 --> 29:21.626 align:middle
Now, it's a slow process,
it's characterized by lags,

29:21.916 --> 29:25.786 align:middle
and that's why it takes a
few years as the inflation--

29:25.926 --> 29:28.976 align:middle
as the unemployment rate falls

29:28.976 --> 29:32.196 align:middle
and even overshoots its
longer-run normal level,

29:32.196 --> 29:35.286 align:middle
it just takes some
time for inflation

29:35.286 --> 29:36.896 align:middle
to get back to 2 percent.

29:37.286 --> 29:42.386 align:middle
But the overshooting
helps it get back faster

29:42.466 --> 29:43.786 align:middle
than it otherwise would.

29:43.786 --> 29:47.466 align:middle
And it's certainly
important for us,

29:47.706 --> 29:49.736 align:middle
and I think our credibility
hinges

29:49.736 --> 29:56.396 align:middle
on defending our inflation
target not only from threats

29:56.396 --> 29:59.596 align:middle
that it rises above, but
also that we not have--

29:59.996 --> 30:03.016 align:middle
that over the medium
term, that we want

30:03.016 --> 30:05.296 align:middle
to see inflation get
back to 2 percent.

30:05.606 --> 30:09.776 align:middle
And we believe the policies
we're following are designed

30:09.776 --> 30:11.456 align:middle
to accomplish that
and will do so.

30:12.866 --> 30:17.716 align:middle
YLAN MUI. Hi, thanks.

30:17.716 --> 30:18.956 align:middle
Ylan from the Washington Post.

30:18.956 --> 30:20.966 align:middle
I want to kind of piggyback on
Binya's question a little bit

30:20.966 --> 30:24.496 align:middle
and bring up the old thresholds
of 6.5 percent unemployment

30:24.496 --> 30:27.506 align:middle
and 2.5 percent inflation
as the period during

30:27.506 --> 30:29.876 align:middle
which the Fed promised to
keep interest rates low.

30:30.886 --> 30:32.186 align:middle
That had sort of assumed--

30:32.186 --> 30:34.516 align:middle
I guess sort of suggested
that the Fed was comfortable

30:34.516 --> 30:37.246 align:middle
with inflation rising
above 2 percent.

30:37.276 --> 30:39.946 align:middle
But you just said moments ago
that you don't want to wait

30:39.946 --> 30:43.666 align:middle
until inflation actually hits
your target before you are ready

30:43.666 --> 30:44.406 align:middle
to lift off.

30:44.666 --> 30:46.576 align:middle
So I wonder if you could
explain that a little bit.

30:46.576 --> 30:49.986 align:middle
Is there a shift in how much
inflation the Fed is actually

30:49.986 --> 30:50.686 align:middle
willing to accept?

30:50.736 --> 30:51.076 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

30:51.076 --> 30:54.396 align:middle
So let me be clear-- 2
percent is our objective.

30:55.036 --> 30:57.826 align:middle
We want to see inflation
go back to 2 percent;

30:57.826 --> 31:00.646 align:middle
2 percent is not a
ceiling on inflation.

31:01.266 --> 31:06.806 align:middle
So we're not trying to push
the inflation rate above 2.

31:06.996 --> 31:10.506 align:middle
It's always our objective
to get back to 2,

31:11.076 --> 31:14.376 align:middle
but 2 percent is not a ceiling.

31:14.626 --> 31:17.466 align:middle
And if it were a
ceiling, you would have

31:17.556 --> 31:21.696 align:middle
to be conducting a
policy that, on average,

31:21.756 --> 31:25.016 align:middle
would hold the inflation
rate below 2 percent.

31:25.276 --> 31:26.636 align:middle
That is not our policy.

31:26.876 --> 31:29.656 align:middle
We want to see the
inflation rate get back

31:29.716 --> 31:32.856 align:middle
to 2 percent as rapidly
as we can.

31:33.346 --> 31:36.016 align:middle
But there are lags in the impact

31:36.016 --> 31:38.616 align:middle
of monetary policy
on the economy.

31:38.996 --> 31:42.766 align:middle
And if we waited until
inflation is back to 2--

31:43.126 --> 31:44.986 align:middle
and that would probably mean

31:44.986 --> 31:50.276 align:middle
that unemployment had declined
well below our estimates

31:50.276 --> 31:54.596 align:middle
of the natural rate-- and
only then did we start to--

31:54.916 --> 31:57.286 align:middle
begin to-- and, you know,

31:57.286 --> 32:01.056 align:middle
the word "tighten" monetary
policy I don't think is really

32:01.056 --> 32:05.386 align:middle
right because we have an
immensely accommodative monetary

32:05.436 --> 32:07.066 align:middle
policy in place.

32:07.066 --> 32:10.016 align:middle
So, let me say, just to begin

32:10.206 --> 32:14.966 align:middle
to diminish the extraordinary
degree of accommodation

32:15.106 --> 32:20.536 align:middle
for monetary policy,
we would be overshoot--

32:20.536 --> 32:24.816 align:middle
we would likely overshoot
substantially our 2 percent

32:24.816 --> 32:28.276 align:middle
objective, and we might
be faced with then having

32:28.316 --> 32:30.506 align:middle
to tighten policy in a way

32:30.656 --> 32:34.126 align:middle
that could be disruptive
to the real economy.

32:34.126 --> 32:36.926 align:middle
And I don't-- I don't think
that's a desirable way

32:36.926 --> 32:38.096 align:middle
to conduct policy.

32:41.576 --> 32:43.366 align:middle
JEANNA SMIALEK.

32:44.066 --> 32:45.616 align:middle
Jeanna Smialek, Bloomberg News.

32:45.616 --> 32:48.546 align:middle
You mentioned earlier that,
you know, there is always going

32:48.546 --> 32:52.046 align:middle
to be some uncertainty in the
global economy, yet it seems

32:52.116 --> 32:54.436 align:middle
that uncertainties are what
kept you from hiking this month.

32:54.436 --> 32:57.626 align:middle
You know, how do you communicate
to markets what is the kind

32:57.626 --> 32:59.596 align:middle
of uncertainty that keeps
you from lifting rates

32:59.596 --> 33:00.476 align:middle
and what is the kind

33:00.476 --> 33:03.576 align:middle
of uncertainty you can overlook
and, you know, move in spite of?

33:03.956 --> 33:04.936 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

33:04.966 --> 33:08.696 align:middle
So, that's a very hard
question, and, you know,

33:08.696 --> 33:13.586 align:middle
it's why we come together and
have very careful evaluations

33:13.586 --> 33:16.036 align:middle
of a wide range of factors.

33:16.406 --> 33:20.236 align:middle
But, at the end of the day, what
we're focused on are two things:

33:20.646 --> 33:25.826 align:middle
the path for employment and
whether or not we feel confident

33:25.866 --> 33:28.446 align:middle
that we're on a road
that will take us

33:28.446 --> 33:32.636 align:middle
to our maximum employment
objective and whether

33:32.636 --> 33:36.796 align:middle
or not we see the risks around
obtaining that as balanced.

33:36.796 --> 33:40.036 align:middle
Of course, there will
be uncertainty around it

33:40.486 --> 33:43.166 align:middle
and whether or not we
have reasonable confidence

33:43.206 --> 33:45.516 align:middle
that inflation, will,
over the medium term,

33:45.816 --> 33:48.136 align:middle
go back to 2 percent.

33:48.466 --> 33:53.616 align:middle
And it's really through that
filter that we're trying

33:53.616 --> 33:55.106 align:middle
to look at uncertainty.

33:55.106 --> 33:57.046 align:middle
Of course, there are
many uncertainties

33:57.046 --> 34:02.776 align:middle
in the global economy, but we're
asking ourselves how economic

34:02.776 --> 34:04.846 align:middle
and financial developments

34:04.846 --> 34:08.846 align:middle
in the global economy affect
the risk to our outlook

34:08.846 --> 34:16.006 align:middle
for our two goals and whether or
not they create unbalanced risks

34:16.006 --> 34:19.826 align:middle
that we want to wait to
resolve, to some extent.

34:20.576 --> 34:22.506 align:middle
PETER BARNES.

34:24.076 --> 34:25.346 align:middle
Hi, Chair Yellen.

34:25.346 --> 34:26.986 align:middle
Peter Barnes, Fox Business.

34:27.276 --> 34:29.746 align:middle
Could you talk about-- a
little bit more specifically

34:29.746 --> 34:32.096 align:middle
about what foreign
developments you discussed

34:32.096 --> 34:34.416 align:middle
in the meeting today, what
you're concerned about?

34:34.416 --> 34:36.936 align:middle
We all assume it
might be China--

34:36.976 --> 34:38.866 align:middle
that was in the July minutes.

34:38.916 --> 34:41.576 align:middle
Are you concerned about the
Chinese economy slowing,

34:41.576 --> 34:42.726 align:middle
the markets there?

34:42.726 --> 34:46.656 align:middle
Do you have any concerns
about the European economy?

34:46.996 --> 34:51.226 align:middle
And then, related
to stock markets,

34:51.226 --> 34:52.186 align:middle
could I ask you how you feel

34:52.186 --> 34:54.446 align:middle
about U.S. equity
markets right now?

34:54.446 --> 34:58.426 align:middle
Because you did talk about your
concerns about them back in May.

34:58.426 --> 35:00.466 align:middle
You saw they were generally
quite high and were worried

35:00.466 --> 35:03.836 align:middle
about potential dangers in
U.S. equity market valuations,

35:03.836 --> 35:05.966 align:middle
but now equity prices
have pulled back.

35:06.196 --> 35:06.456 align:middle
Thank you.

35:07.636 --> 35:08.096 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

35:08.096 --> 35:10.306 align:middle
So, with respect to
global developments,

35:10.306 --> 35:15.846 align:middle
we reviewed developments in all
important areas of the world,

35:15.846 --> 35:20.106 align:middle
but we're focused particularly
on China and emerging markets.

35:20.546 --> 35:25.016 align:middle
Now, we've long expected,
as most analysts have,

35:25.446 --> 35:30.796 align:middle
to see some slowing in
Chinese growth over time

35:30.796 --> 35:33.076 align:middle
as they rebalance their economy.

35:33.526 --> 35:35.516 align:middle
And they have planned that,

35:35.516 --> 35:38.996 align:middle
and I think there are
no surprises there.

35:39.476 --> 35:43.796 align:middle
The question is whether or
not there might be a risk

35:43.986 --> 35:49.966 align:middle
of a more abrupt slowdown
than most analysts expect.

35:50.496 --> 35:55.606 align:middle
And I think developments that
we saw in financial markets

35:55.606 --> 36:01.416 align:middle
in August in part reflected
concerns that Chinese--

36:01.626 --> 36:05.136 align:middle
there was downside risk to
Chinese economic performance

36:05.426 --> 36:09.976 align:middle
and perhaps concerns
about the deftness

36:09.976 --> 36:13.826 align:middle
with which policymakers were
addressing those concerns.

36:14.296 --> 36:19.546 align:middle
In addition, we saw a very
substantial downward pressure

36:19.546 --> 36:23.056 align:middle
on oil prices and
commodity markets,

36:23.456 --> 36:27.676 align:middle
and those developments have
had a significant impact

36:27.966 --> 36:30.366 align:middle
on many emerging
market economies

36:30.646 --> 36:34.396 align:middle
that are important producers
of commodities as well

36:34.396 --> 36:37.946 align:middle
as more advanced
countries, including Canada,

36:37.946 --> 36:41.166 align:middle
which is an important
trading partner of ours

36:41.486 --> 36:44.066 align:middle
that has been negatively
affected

36:44.066 --> 36:49.046 align:middle
by declining commodity prices,
declining energy prices.

36:49.046 --> 36:52.806 align:middle
Now, there are a lot of
countries that are net importers

36:52.806 --> 36:56.646 align:middle
of energy that are positively
affected by those developments,

36:57.066 --> 37:00.556 align:middle
but emerging markets--
important emerging markets--

37:00.556 --> 37:04.156 align:middle
have been negatively affected
by those developments.

37:04.486 --> 37:10.956 align:middle
And we've seen significant
outflows of capital

37:10.956 --> 37:14.996 align:middle
from those countries, pressures
on their exchange rates,

37:15.286 --> 37:19.196 align:middle
and concerns about their
performance going forward.

37:19.196 --> 37:23.356 align:middle
So a lot of our focus has
been on risks around China,

37:23.356 --> 37:27.526 align:middle
but not just China-- emerging
markets, more generally,

37:27.526 --> 37:30.986 align:middle
and how they may spill
over to the United States.

37:30.986 --> 37:33.796 align:middle
In terms of thinking about
financial developments

37:33.796 --> 37:37.026 align:middle
and our reaction to
them, I think a lot

37:37.026 --> 37:39.556 align:middle
of the financial
developments were really--

37:39.866 --> 37:42.886 align:middle
so, we don't want to
respond to market turbulence.

37:42.886 --> 37:46.526 align:middle
The Fed should not be
responding to the ups and downs

37:46.766 --> 37:50.626 align:middle
of the markets, and it is
certainly not our policy

37:50.686 --> 37:51.396 align:middle
to do so.

37:51.816 --> 37:56.586 align:middle
But when there are significant
financial developments,

37:56.866 --> 38:00.926 align:middle
it's incumbent on us to ask
ourselves what is causing them.

38:01.276 --> 38:05.006 align:middle
And, of course, while we can't
know for sure, it seemed to us

38:05.006 --> 38:10.546 align:middle
as though concerns about the
global economic outlook were

38:10.546 --> 38:13.346 align:middle
drivers of those
financial developments.

38:13.766 --> 38:18.576 align:middle
And so they have concerned us
in part because they take us

38:18.606 --> 38:21.826 align:middle
to the global outlook and
how that will affect us.

38:22.186 --> 38:27.536 align:middle
And, to some extent, look,
we have seen a tightening

38:27.536 --> 38:29.296 align:middle
of financial conditions during--

38:29.296 --> 38:32.206 align:middle
as I mentioned, during
the intermeeting period.

38:32.676 --> 38:36.496 align:middle
So the stock market adjustment,

38:37.056 --> 38:40.636 align:middle
combined with a somewhat
stronger dollar

38:40.866 --> 38:44.576 align:middle
and higher risk spreads, does
represent some tightening

38:44.576 --> 38:46.116 align:middle
of financial conditions.

38:46.526 --> 38:52.396 align:middle
Now, in and of itself, it's
not the end of story in terms

38:52.396 --> 38:54.406 align:middle
of our policy because
we have to put a lot

38:54.406 --> 38:56.606 align:middle
of different pieces together.

38:56.956 --> 39:00.766 align:middle
We are looking at, as I
emphasized, a U.S. economy

39:00.766 --> 39:06.086 align:middle
that has been performing well
and impressing us by the pace

39:06.086 --> 39:09.976 align:middle
at which it's creating jobs and
the strength of domestic demand.

39:10.576 --> 39:13.186 align:middle
So we have that.

39:13.186 --> 39:19.216 align:middle
We have some concerns
about negative impacts

39:19.416 --> 39:23.236 align:middle
from global developments
and some tightening

39:23.236 --> 39:24.606 align:middle
of financial conditions.

39:24.606 --> 39:27.686 align:middle
We're trying to put all of
that together in a picture.

39:27.686 --> 39:31.076 align:middle
I think, importantly, we say
in our statement that in spite

39:31.076 --> 39:34.586 align:middle
of all of this, we
continue to view the risks

39:35.016 --> 39:38.586 align:middle
to economic activity and
labor markets as balanced.

39:38.586 --> 39:40.676 align:middle
So it's a lot of
different pieces,

39:40.896 --> 39:44.756 align:middle
different cross currents, some
strengthening the outlook,

39:44.756 --> 39:49.056 align:middle
some creating concerns, but
overall, no significant change

39:49.056 --> 39:50.406 align:middle
in the economic outlook.

39:51.706 --> 39:53.296 align:middle
ANN SAPHIR.

39:59.026 --> 40:02.066 align:middle
Ann Saphir with Reuters.

40:02.516 --> 40:05.666 align:middle
Just to piggyback on the
global considerations--

40:05.966 --> 40:09.336 align:middle
as you say, the U.S.
economy has been growing.

40:09.516 --> 40:12.696 align:middle
Are you worried that, given
the global interconnectedness,

40:12.796 --> 40:16.676 align:middle
the low inflation globally,
all of the other concerns

40:16.676 --> 40:19.776 align:middle
that you just spoke about,
that you may never escape

40:19.976 --> 40:22.476 align:middle
from this zero lower
bound situation?

40:23.456 --> 40:24.256 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

40:24.256 --> 40:27.526 align:middle
So I would be very-- I
would be very surprised

40:27.596 --> 40:28.716 align:middle
if that's the case.

40:28.716 --> 40:31.716 align:middle
That is not the way
I see the outlook

40:31.966 --> 40:34.476 align:middle
or the way the Committee
sees the outlook.

40:34.896 --> 40:37.156 align:middle
Can I completely rule it out?

40:37.156 --> 40:39.036 align:middle
I can't completely rule it out.

40:39.116 --> 40:42.806 align:middle
But, really, that's an
extreme downside risk

40:42.856 --> 40:49.236 align:middle
that in no way is near
the center of my outlook.

40:51.116 --> 40:53.436 align:middle
MICHAEL MCKEE.

40:54.186 --> 40:56.136 align:middle
Michael McKee from Bloomberg
Radio and Television.

40:56.526 --> 40:58.426 align:middle
If the economy develops
as the Summary

40:58.426 --> 41:00.116 align:middle
of Economic Projections
suggests,

41:00.116 --> 41:02.576 align:middle
you will see improvement
in labor markets,

41:02.576 --> 41:05.396 align:middle
but it won't push
inflation up any faster.

41:05.866 --> 41:08.106 align:middle
So I'm wondering
what the argument is

41:08.416 --> 41:11.716 align:middle
for raising rates this year
as suggested by the dot plot,

41:11.936 --> 41:14.606 align:middle
because even allowing for
long and variable lags,

41:14.886 --> 41:17.186 align:middle
you're not forecasting
an inflation problem

41:17.186 --> 41:19.516 align:middle
that would seem to suggest
the need for a steeper

41:19.516 --> 41:25.446 align:middle
and faster rate path for
at least a couple of years.

41:25.966 --> 41:26.866 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

41:28.416 --> 41:35.976 align:middle
So, if we maintain a highly
accommodative monetary policy

41:36.596 --> 41:42.846 align:middle
for a very long time from
here and the economy performs

41:42.846 --> 41:47.746 align:middle
as we expect-- namely, it's
strong and the risks that are

41:47.746 --> 41:50.136 align:middle
out there don't materialize--

41:50.616 --> 41:56.006 align:middle
my concern will be that we
will have much more tightening

41:56.006 --> 42:00.066 align:middle
in labor markets than you
see in these projections.

42:00.456 --> 42:04.466 align:middle
And the lags will
be probably slow,

42:04.846 --> 42:07.936 align:middle
but eventually we
will find ourselves

42:07.976 --> 42:12.206 align:middle
with a substantial overshoot
of our inflation objective,

42:12.566 --> 42:17.226 align:middle
and then we'll be forced into
a kind of stop-go policy.

42:17.296 --> 42:19.576 align:middle
We will have pushed the economy

42:20.446 --> 42:23.306 align:middle
so far it will have
become overheated,

42:23.716 --> 42:27.746 align:middle
and we will then have to
tighten policy more abruptly

42:27.746 --> 42:28.506 align:middle
than we'd like.

42:28.846 --> 42:32.416 align:middle
And instead of having
slow, steady growth,

42:32.416 --> 42:37.146 align:middle
improvement in the labor market,
and continued improvement

42:37.146 --> 42:39.776 align:middle
and good performance
in the labor market,

42:40.166 --> 42:44.436 align:middle
I don't think it's good
policy to have to then slam

42:44.436 --> 42:53.656 align:middle
on the brakes and risk a
downturn in the economy.

42:53.826 --> 42:55.826 align:middle
MICHAEL DERBY.

42:56.366 --> 42:58.876 align:middle
Mike Derby with Dow
Jones Newswires.

42:58.876 --> 43:02.896 align:middle
One of your colleagues in
the dot plots said they'd

43:02.896 --> 43:04.506 align:middle
like to see negative
interest rates--

43:04.506 --> 43:07.036 align:middle
didn't expect to see that.

43:07.036 --> 43:11.836 align:middle
What do you make of
negative interest rates

43:11.876 --> 43:14.876 align:middle
as a potential source of new
stimulus if the Fed were to have

43:14.916 --> 43:17.856 align:middle
to do something more, you know,
as opposed to maybe going to QE?

43:18.266 --> 43:21.906 align:middle
Does negative interest rates
have any part-- does it--

43:22.176 --> 43:24.746 align:middle
is it-- should it be part of
the Fed's toolkit, essentially?

43:24.896 --> 43:25.156 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

43:25.316 --> 43:26.626 align:middle
So, let me be clear

43:26.626 --> 43:28.996 align:middle
that negative interest
rates was not something

43:28.996 --> 43:32.016 align:middle
that we considered very
seriously at all today.

43:32.016 --> 43:35.446 align:middle
It's-- was not one of
our main policy options.

43:35.826 --> 43:41.646 align:middle
But one participant
in the Committee would

43:41.646 --> 43:46.256 align:middle
like to see additional
accommodation, is concerned

43:46.256 --> 43:47.966 align:middle
by the inflation outlook,

43:48.246 --> 43:51.006 align:middle
and thinks that we need
additional stimulus,

43:51.316 --> 43:56.796 align:middle
additional accommodation to
provide that, and proposed doing

43:56.796 --> 44:00.346 align:middle
so by moving interest
rates negative.

44:00.386 --> 44:03.736 align:middle
That's something we've seen
in several European countries.

44:04.116 --> 44:06.366 align:middle
It's not something we
talked about today.

44:06.366 --> 44:11.696 align:middle
Look, if-- I don't expect that
we're going to be in a path

44:11.696 --> 44:13.986 align:middle
of providing additional
accommodation,

44:14.286 --> 44:17.436 align:middle
but if the outlook were to
change in a way that most

44:17.436 --> 44:22.006 align:middle
of my colleagues and I do not
expect and we found ourselves

44:22.286 --> 44:25.596 align:middle
with a weak economy that
needed additional stimulus,

44:25.896 --> 44:28.106 align:middle
we would look at all
of our available tools,

44:28.376 --> 44:31.216 align:middle
and that would be something
that we would evaluate

44:31.216 --> 44:32.656 align:middle
in that kind of context.

44:33.436 --> 44:36.936 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

44:37.016 --> 44:38.656 align:middle
Marty Crutsinger,
Associated Press.

44:39.776 --> 44:41.696 align:middle
In July, when you
were talking to us,

44:41.786 --> 44:44.256 align:middle
you said that you
yourself expected

44:44.566 --> 44:47.676 align:middle
to see the first rate hike
before the end of the year.

44:47.926 --> 44:50.256 align:middle
Is that still your expectation?

44:50.536 --> 44:53.296 align:middle
And also, when you talked
about the developments

44:53.296 --> 44:58.296 align:middle
in financial markets and what
caused the August turbulence--

44:58.296 --> 44:59.686 align:middle
you mentioned China
and other things--

45:00.056 --> 45:02.866 align:middle
you did mention the prospects
of a Fed rate increase.

45:02.866 --> 45:04.906 align:middle
Do you think that
played a role as well?

45:05.456 --> 45:07.326 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

45:07.646 --> 45:11.316 align:middle
So you ask me about my own
expectation, and I'd say--

45:11.736 --> 45:15.906 align:middle
I don't want to-- I speak on
behalf of the Committee and try

45:15.906 --> 45:21.606 align:middle
to explain Committee decisions,
and we don't identify who's

45:21.606 --> 45:25.446 align:middle
who in terms of our
projections of the funds rate,

45:25.776 --> 45:29.236 align:middle
and I don't want to change
that and have the focus be

45:29.576 --> 45:33.536 align:middle
on my personal views
on the path.

45:33.536 --> 45:38.296 align:middle
You know, I have characterized
the Committee view as, you know,

45:38.416 --> 45:44.356 align:middle
a forecast that will
likely, if it prevails,

45:44.356 --> 45:46.236 align:middle
if that's how the
economy evolves,

45:46.506 --> 45:49.756 align:middle
call for a funds rate
increase later this year.

45:50.036 --> 45:52.776 align:middle
And I think that's
a fair summary

45:53.236 --> 45:56.636 align:middle
of the Committee's
assessment of things.

45:57.176 --> 46:00.796 align:middle
You asked me, I think,
also about uncertainty

46:00.796 --> 46:02.336 align:middle
about our own policies?

46:02.336 --> 46:02.936 align:middle
MARTIN CRUTSINGER.

46:03.006 --> 46:04.266 align:middle
Well, no, not uncertainty,
just the fact

46:04.266 --> 46:05.456 align:middle
that the market turbulence--

46:05.546 --> 46:08.726 align:middle
a lot of people in August said
part of that was a concern

46:08.726 --> 46:10.736 align:middle
about the Fed was about
to raise interest rates,

46:11.356 --> 46:14.366 align:middle
and you didn't mention that as
one reason for the turbulence.

46:15.106 --> 46:15.446 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

46:15.446 --> 46:17.236 align:middle
So I think the main drivers

46:17.236 --> 46:21.146 align:middle
of the turbulence
have been concerns

46:21.146 --> 46:24.646 align:middle
about the global outlook,
that's how I read it.

46:24.646 --> 46:25.906 align:middle
But I know that, of course,

46:25.966 --> 46:29.116 align:middle
there is uncertainty
about Fed policy.

46:29.426 --> 46:33.756 align:middle
As I mentioned, we're well aware
that there's been a huge focus

46:33.756 --> 46:35.706 align:middle
on the decision today.

46:36.056 --> 46:38.566 align:middle
And, you know, I would
ask you to appreciate

46:38.566 --> 46:42.766 align:middle
that there are a lot of
cross-currents in economic

46:42.766 --> 46:48.206 align:middle
and financial developments that
we need to take into account

46:48.476 --> 46:52.566 align:middle
in deciding on what the
appropriate course of policy is.

46:52.896 --> 46:56.826 align:middle
And we don't make continuous
decisions every single day

46:56.826 --> 47:00.556 align:middle
about our policy, we
meet periodically.

47:00.896 --> 47:06.486 align:middle
We do our darndest to pull
together the best analysis we

47:06.486 --> 47:12.106 align:middle
can and to exchange views and to
arrive at Committee decisions.

47:12.416 --> 47:15.746 align:middle
I do understand that, during
this intermeeting period,

47:16.296 --> 47:21.776 align:middle
that every word that an FOMC
member has said has been parsed

47:21.776 --> 47:27.076 align:middle
for its potential implications
for what our decision will be.

47:27.316 --> 47:31.576 align:middle
I think it's, you know-- that's
an unfortunate state of affairs,

47:31.926 --> 47:36.506 align:middle
but I understand, and I think
it's natural when you're

47:36.506 --> 47:41.116 align:middle
at a point when conditions may
be falling in place for there

47:41.116 --> 47:43.386 align:middle
to be a shift in policy.

47:43.596 --> 47:48.056 align:middle
It's natural that that
should happen, and it does,

47:48.056 --> 47:49.646 align:middle
to some extent, contribute

47:49.646 --> 47:51.786 align:middle
to uncertainty in
financial markets.

47:53.636 --> 47:55.786 align:middle
MICHELLE FLEURY.

47:56.336 --> 47:58.236 align:middle
Hi, Michelle Fleury, BBC News.

47:58.626 --> 48:00.256 align:middle
You talked a lot about
the strong dollar.

48:00.256 --> 48:04.446 align:middle
I wondered, do you see your
policy actions affecting

48:04.836 --> 48:05.426 align:middle
the dollar?

48:05.426 --> 48:06.456 align:middle
Is it something you consider

48:06.456 --> 48:08.066 align:middle
when you are making
your policy decisions?

48:10.176 --> 48:10.776 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

48:11.226 --> 48:14.236 align:middle
So, monetary policy--

48:14.236 --> 48:20.216 align:middle
U.S. monetary policy is
directed toward trying

48:20.216 --> 48:25.116 align:middle
to achieve the goals the
Congress has laid out for us.

48:26.426 --> 48:30.136 align:middle
When we-- when monetary
policy tightens

48:30.186 --> 48:34.826 align:middle
and interest rates rise,
it commonly is the case,

48:34.826 --> 48:37.666 align:middle
either when it happens
or in expectation--

48:38.246 --> 48:41.676 align:middle
the expectation that
that's coming--

48:41.836 --> 48:46.886 align:middle
interest rate differentials
globally do tend

48:46.886 --> 48:51.476 align:middle
to reduce capital flows that
have impacts on exchange rates.

48:51.886 --> 48:56.606 align:middle
So monetary policy
often has some effect

48:56.606 --> 49:01.136 align:middle
on the exchange rate,
and it's not, in my view,

49:01.136 --> 49:04.356 align:middle
the main channel by which
monetary policy works.

49:04.736 --> 49:07.126 align:middle
It's one of a number
of different channels

49:07.386 --> 49:12.486 align:middle
by which monetary policy works,
but it does have some impact

49:12.486 --> 49:14.776 align:middle
on exchange rates,
and, of course, yes,

49:14.776 --> 49:16.456 align:middle
we need to take that
into account.

49:17.856 --> 49:20.846 align:middle
GREG ROBB.

49:21.596 --> 49:22.856 align:middle
Greg Robb from MarketWatch.

49:23.316 --> 49:27.106 align:middle
I want to see if you would shift
gears a little bit and talk

49:27.106 --> 49:28.056 align:middle
about the housing market.

49:28.056 --> 49:30.896 align:middle
You say in the statement, the
housing market has improved.

49:31.526 --> 49:34.296 align:middle
How much are you counting
on the housing market

49:34.296 --> 49:35.436 align:middle
for growth going forward,

49:35.646 --> 49:38.756 align:middle
especially since the
Committee sees rates rising?

49:39.596 --> 49:44.006 align:middle
Thanks. CHAIR YELLEN.

49:44.006 --> 49:47.486 align:middle
So we are envisioning
further improvements

49:47.486 --> 49:48.656 align:middle
in the housing market.

49:48.656 --> 49:52.066 align:middle
It remains very depressed--

49:52.346 --> 49:57.266 align:middle
housing starts below
levels that seem consistent

49:57.266 --> 49:59.116 align:middle
with underlying demographics,

49:59.926 --> 50:06.886 align:middle
especially in an economy that's
creating jobs, and we have lots

50:07.506 --> 50:09.616 align:middle
of people who are
still doubled up,

50:09.616 --> 50:15.576 align:middle
and demand for housing should
be there and should materialize

50:15.576 --> 50:19.796 align:middle
as the job market improves
and income growth improves.

50:19.876 --> 50:23.996 align:middle
So, are we counting on it?

50:23.996 --> 50:27.466 align:middle
Housing is now a very small
sector of the economy.

50:27.866 --> 50:30.526 align:middle
It is not the driver of--

50:30.866 --> 50:34.796 align:middle
it is not the key
driver in my own forecast

50:35.036 --> 50:38.586 align:middle
of ongoing improvements
in the U.S. economy.

50:38.986 --> 50:41.546 align:middle
It plays a supporting role,

50:41.546 --> 50:46.976 align:middle
but consumer spending is the
main driver, bolstered by,

50:46.976 --> 50:51.496 align:middle
you know, a decent outlook
for investment spending.

50:51.926 --> 50:56.976 align:middle
But I would continue to
expect housing to improve.

50:57.426 --> 51:02.386 align:middle
And, remember, we're
envisioning, if things go

51:02.386 --> 51:10.406 align:middle
as we anticipate, a pretty
gradual path of increases

51:10.406 --> 51:13.276 align:middle
in short-term interest
rates over time.

51:13.616 --> 51:17.426 align:middle
To some extent, that's already
embodied in longer term rates.

51:17.776 --> 51:21.256 align:middle
On the other hand,
as time passes

51:21.596 --> 51:25.986 align:middle
and we move beyond the window
in which short rates are zero,

51:26.296 --> 51:29.316 align:middle
it will be natural for
long rates to rise some.

51:29.536 --> 51:31.236 align:middle
And, of course, we recognize

51:31.236 --> 51:35.266 align:middle
that the housing market is
sensitive to mortgage rates.

51:35.266 --> 51:37.516 align:middle
It's a-- it is an
important factor.

51:37.826 --> 51:40.096 align:middle
But that's something that,
of course, we are taking

51:40.096 --> 51:41.806 align:middle
into account in thinking

51:41.806 --> 51:44.176 align:middle
about what's the
appropriate path of policy.

51:44.486 --> 51:46.516 align:middle
NANCY MARSHALL-GENZER.

51:46.916 --> 51:49.946 align:middle
Nancy Marshall-Genzer
with Marketplace.

51:50.326 --> 51:51.366 align:middle
You mentioned you've
gotten a lot

51:51.366 --> 51:54.966 align:middle
of unsolicited advice--
the folks outside.

51:55.006 --> 51:56.346 align:middle
But there is another side

51:56.346 --> 51:59.116 align:middle
that says the Fed should
raise interest rates

51:59.426 --> 52:01.096 align:middle
because keeping rates so long--

52:01.166 --> 52:05.356 align:middle
so low for so long has actually
exacerbated the wealth gap.

52:05.416 --> 52:07.506 align:middle
Do you think the Fed has
widened the wealth gap

52:08.086 --> 52:09.636 align:middle
with its low interest
rate policy?

52:09.636 --> 52:11.956 align:middle
These people say low
interest rates mainly benefit

52:11.986 --> 52:12.436 align:middle
the wealthy.

52:12.916 --> 52:13.536 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

52:13.536 --> 52:17.276 align:middle
Well, I guess I really
don't see it that way.

52:17.276 --> 52:22.336 align:middle
It is true that interest
rates affect asset prices,

52:22.386 --> 52:26.696 align:middle
but they have a complex
effect through balance sheets,

52:26.696 --> 52:29.376 align:middle
through liabilities and assets.

52:29.456 --> 52:33.826 align:middle
To me, the main thing that an
accommodative monetary policy

52:33.826 --> 52:36.266 align:middle
does is put people back to work.

52:36.766 --> 52:44.776 align:middle
And since income inequality is
surely exacerbated by a high--

52:44.776 --> 52:48.046 align:middle
having high unemployment
and a weak job market

52:48.396 --> 52:51.866 align:middle
that has the most
profound negative effects

52:52.116 --> 52:56.796 align:middle
on the most vulnerable
individuals, to me,

52:57.136 --> 53:01.816 align:middle
putting people back to work
and seeing a strengthening

53:01.816 --> 53:08.096 align:middle
of the labor market that has
a disproportionately favorable

53:08.096 --> 53:13.006 align:middle
effect on vulnerable
portions of our population,

53:13.176 --> 53:16.246 align:middle
that's not something that
increases income inequality.

53:16.756 --> 53:19.756 align:middle
There have been a number
of studies that have done--

53:19.896 --> 53:23.486 align:middle
been done recently that
have tried to take account

53:23.486 --> 53:27.826 align:middle
of many different ways
in which monetary policy,

53:28.006 --> 53:30.196 align:middle
acting through different parts

53:30.196 --> 53:35.216 align:middle
of the transmission
mechanism, affect inequality.

53:35.576 --> 53:38.796 align:middle
And there's a lot of
guesswork involved,

53:38.796 --> 53:41.896 align:middle
and different analyses can
come up with different things.

53:41.896 --> 53:48.056 align:middle
But a pretty recent paper that's
quite comprehensive concludes

53:48.536 --> 53:52.566 align:middle
that Fed policy has not
exacerbated income inequality.

53:53.306 --> 53:56.386 align:middle
JON PRIOR.

53:56.956 --> 54:06.346 align:middle
Thanks. What-- I'm Jon
Prior with Politico.

54:06.446 --> 54:10.506 align:middle
What role did a possible
government shutdown this year

54:10.506 --> 54:13.606 align:middle
play in your decision today
to delay the rate hike,

54:14.046 --> 54:15.526 align:middle
and what would you
say to lawmakers

54:15.526 --> 54:17.146 align:middle
who are pursuing this
strategy yet again?

54:17.306 --> 54:17.566 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

54:17.886 --> 54:21.606 align:middle
Well, it played absolutely
no role in our decision.

54:22.176 --> 54:26.206 align:middle
I believe it's the
responsibility of Congress

54:26.236 --> 54:29.106 align:middle
to pass a budget, to
fund the government,

54:29.366 --> 54:33.906 align:middle
to deal with a debt ceiling so
that America pays its bills.

54:35.356 --> 54:40.596 align:middle
We have a good recovery in place
that's really making progress,

54:40.596 --> 54:45.296 align:middle
and to see Congress take
actions that would endanger

54:45.296 --> 54:50.126 align:middle
that progress-- I think that
would be more than unfortunate.

54:50.126 --> 54:52.796 align:middle
So, to me, that's
Congress's job.

54:53.166 --> 54:58.036 align:middle
Congress charged us with
forming an economic outlook

54:58.376 --> 55:01.716 align:middle
that is focused on
the medium term

55:01.716 --> 55:06.646 align:middle
and taking appropriate policy
actions based on that outlook,

55:06.906 --> 55:09.666 align:middle
and that's what we
have done in the past

55:09.666 --> 55:11.536 align:middle
and will continue
to do going forward.

55:15.156 --> 55:17.086 align:middle
STEVE BECKNER.

55:19.336 --> 55:21.136 align:middle
Madam Chair, Steve
Beckner of MNI.

55:21.436 --> 55:25.206 align:middle
You said in your opening
statement that the Fed's policy

55:25.206 --> 55:27.846 align:middle
of maintaining a
large balance sheet--

55:27.846 --> 55:30.396 align:middle
by not starting to
shrink that balance sheet

55:30.396 --> 55:34.396 align:middle
by curtailing reinvestments
and rollovers--

55:34.816 --> 55:38.526 align:middle
that helps add to your
accommodative monetary stance

55:38.746 --> 55:42.656 align:middle
on top of the near-zero
federal funds rate.

55:43.056 --> 55:46.076 align:middle
By delaying rate
hikes, logically,

55:46.076 --> 55:51.536 align:middle
are you not also delaying
reducing the balance sheet?

55:51.536 --> 55:55.046 align:middle
A year ago, the FOMC said it
would not start shrinking the

55:55.046 --> 55:56.596 align:middle
balance sheet and still it's--

55:56.866 --> 55:58.676 align:middle
until it started
raising the funds rate.

55:59.006 --> 56:03.556 align:middle
So is it a concern that you
are delaying normalization

56:03.556 --> 56:04.726 align:middle
of the balance sheet?

56:04.726 --> 56:07.686 align:middle
Was that an issue that you
and your colleagues discussed?

56:07.846 --> 56:08.206 align:middle
Thank you.

56:09.276 --> 56:10.036 align:middle
CHAIR YELLEN.

56:10.496 --> 56:13.846 align:middle
We have been-- and this
was reported in the minutes

56:13.846 --> 56:17.726 align:middle
of our July meeting--
we have been discussing

56:17.726 --> 56:19.366 align:middle
reinvestment policy.

56:20.206 --> 56:24.016 align:middle
Our normalization
principles indicated

56:24.446 --> 56:30.166 align:middle
that we would not
begin to either reduce

56:30.166 --> 56:36.846 align:middle
or eliminate reinvestments
until after we have begun

56:36.846 --> 56:39.096 align:middle
to raise the federal funds rate.

56:39.756 --> 56:43.686 align:middle
Our principles said
that the exact timing

56:43.686 --> 56:48.966 align:middle
of that would depend on economic
and financial conditions

56:48.966 --> 56:52.986 align:middle
and our evaluation of them,
and that guidance continues

56:52.986 --> 56:55.796 align:middle
to be accurate-- we don't
have anything further on it.

56:56.086 --> 57:01.376 align:middle
But it is certainly true that
we have committed to wait

57:01.526 --> 57:05.506 align:middle
to begin running down
our balance sheet

57:05.506 --> 57:09.706 align:middle
until after we've begun the
process of normalization.

57:10.126 --> 57:14.886 align:middle
So, yes, if we defer, this
is not a very large matter

57:14.886 --> 57:18.326 align:middle
that we're talking about from
a stimulus point of view.

57:18.746 --> 57:21.146 align:middle
But it is, to some extent,

57:21.146 --> 57:25.356 align:middle
true that if we delay raising
the rate, it probably--

57:26.006 --> 57:30.746 align:middle
maybe delays the timing at
which that process will begin.

57:31.036 --> 57:36.226 align:middle
But there's no fixed-- we've not
given some fixed amount of time

57:36.226 --> 57:38.566 align:middle
at so many months
after we start.

57:38.836 --> 57:43.826 align:middle
And we're continuing to discuss
what the appropriate timing

57:43.826 --> 57:45.856 align:middle
would be of that policy

57:46.086 --> 58:00.086 align:middle
and haven't made any further
decisions on that just yet.

